window.PET_DATA["knowledge/grammar-02-conditionals.json"] = {"id":"grammar-02","type":"grammar","title":"Conditionals","objective":"Use zero, first and second conditionals correctly, including unless and time clauses.","sections":[{"heading":"Zero & First Conditional","body":"**Zero conditional** (general truths): `if + present simple, present simple` — `If you heat ice, it melts.`\n**First conditional** (real future possibilities): `if + present simple, will + verb` — `If it rains tomorrow, we will stay at home.`","bodyZh":"零条件句表示普遍事实（if + 一般现在时，一般现在时）；第一条件句表示将来真实可能发生的事（if + 一般现在时，will + 动词）。","examples":[{"en":"If you study every day, your English will improve quickly.","zh":"如果你每天学习，你的英语会进步很快。"},{"en":"What will you do if you miss the bus?","zh":"如果你错过公交车，你会怎么办？"}],"tip":"Never put `will` in the if-clause: `If it will rain` is wrong. The if-clause takes the present simple."},{"heading":"Second Conditional","body":"**Second conditional** (imaginary or unlikely present/future): `if + past simple, would + verb` — `If I had more money, I would travel around the world.`\nWith `be`, use `were` for all persons in formal English: `If I were you, I would accept the job.`","bodyZh":"第二条件句表示想象中或不太可能的情况（if + 过去时，would + 动词）。正式用法中 be 动词一律用 were。","examples":[{"en":"If I lived near the sea, I would swim every morning.","zh":"如果我住在海边，我会每天早上游泳。"},{"en":"She would be happier if she had more free time.","zh":"如果她有更多空闲时间，她会更开心。"}],"tip":"`If I were you, I'd...` is the standard way to give advice in Speaking Part 4."},{"heading":"unless & time clauses","body":"`unless` means 'if ... not': `You won't pass unless you practise.` (= if you don't practise)\nAfter `when, as soon as, before, after, until` referring to the future, use the present simple: `I'll call you when I arrive.` (not 'when I will arrive')","bodyZh":"unless 意为「如果不」；在表示将来的 when、as soon as、before、after、until 从句中用一般现在时。","examples":[{"en":"We'll have a picnic unless it rains.","zh":"除非下雨，否则我们去野餐。"},{"en":"As soon as the film finishes, I'll go home.","zh":"电影一结束我就回家。"}],"tip":"The same 'no will' rule applies to unless and all time clauses: the future idea is shown in the main clause."}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"If I will see him, I will tell him.","right":"If I see him, I will tell him.","why":"No 'will' in the if-clause."},{"wrong":"If I would have time, I would help.","right":"If I had time, I would help.","why":"Second conditional uses past simple in the if-clause, not would."},{"wrong":"You will be late until you hurry.","right":"You will be late unless you hurry.","why":"'until' is about time; 'unless' means 'if not'."}],"summary":{"keyPoints":["Zero: if + present, present (facts).","First: if + present, will + verb (real future).","Second: if + past, would + verb (imaginary).","unless = if ... not; time clauses take present simple."],"mustKnow":["if it rains, I will...","if I had..., I would...","if I were you","unless"]},"linkedPractice":["reading"],"reviewItems":[]};
